+## Database tuning
+
+The bundled PostgreSQL and TimescaleDB read the same `POSTGRES_*`
+variables from `.env`, active from the first start, with defaults sized
+for **about 100 machines** on a modest host; autovacuum runs more often
+than stock (scale factors 0.05 / 0.02) so the nightly purges don't leave
+dead rows behind. Change a value in `.env`, then `docker compose up -d`
+(the database restarts with it). With your own PostgreSQL set the same
+parameters in its `postgresql.conf`.
 
-`max_connections` needs your own `command:` override on `db`.
+Rough values per fleet (at default intervals; the database's share of
+the host's RAM in the first row):
+
+| Variable (PostgreSQL setting) | ~100 (default) | ~500 | ~1 000 | ~5 000 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| RAM for the database | ~1 GB | 2–4 GB | 4–8 GB | 16–32 GB |
+| `POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS` (`shared_buffers`, ~25 % of it) | 256MB | 1GB | 2GB | 6GB |
+| `POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE` (`effective_cache_size`, ~75 %) | 768MB | 3GB | 6GB | 18GB |
+| `POSTGRES_WORK_MEM` (`work_mem`) | 8MB | 16MB | 16MB | 32MB |
+| `POSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM` (`maintenance_work_mem`) | 128MB | 256MB | 512MB | 1GB |
+| `POSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS` (`max_connections`) | 100 | 100 | 150 | 300 |
+| `DB_POOL_SIZE` / `DB_MAX_OVERFLOW` (web) | 10 / 20 | 15 / 30 | 20 / 40 | 30 / 60 |
+| worker replicas × `CELERY_WORKER_CONCURRENCY` | 1 × 10 | 2 × 10 | 3 × 10 | 6–8 × 20 |
+| TimescaleDB: `TIMESCALEDB_MAX_BACKGROUND_WORKERS` | 4 | 4 | 8 | 16 |
+| TimescaleDB: `TIMESCALEDB_MAX_WORKER_PROCESSES` (`max_worker_processes`) | 16 | 16 | 24 | 32 |
+
+How the numbers come about, to adjust for your host:
+
+- **`max_connections`** ≥ `DB_POOL_SIZE + DB_MAX_OVERFLOW` + worker
+  replicas × concurrency + ~10 (beat, migrate, psql). Re-check whenever
+  you add workers.
+- **`shared_buffers`** about a quarter of the RAM the database may use,
+  **`effective_cache_size`** about three quarters (it only tells the
+  planner how much the OS caches; it allocates nothing).
+- **`work_mem`** is per sort/hash per query, so it multiplies with
+  connections: keep `work_mem × max_connections` well under the
+  database's RAM.
+- **`maintenance_work_mem`** speeds up autovacuum and index builds
+  (migrations); up to ~1GB helps on big tables.
+- **TimescaleDB background workers** run its compression and retention
+  jobs; `max_worker_processes` must cover them plus PostgreSQL's own
+  parallel workers (8) and a few spare.
+- Disk: SSD/NVMe. The database is mostly writes (samples) and big
+  deletes or chunk drops at night; see [Disk growth](#-disk-growth).
+
+Signs it needs more: `SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;` close to
+`max_connections`; slow pages with nothing failing; autovacuum constantly
+running on the sample tables (`pg_stat_user_tables.last_autovacuum`).
 
