+## 🧩 What runs: Compose profiles
+
+`web`, `worker`, `beat`, `migrate` and Redis always run. Everything else is
+a [Compose profile](https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/profiles/),
+switched on by `COMPOSE_PROFILES` in `.env` (read by `docker compose`
+itself, so no `--profile` or `-f` flags are ever needed):
+
+| Profile | Service | What it is |
+|---|---|---|
+| `postgres` | `db` | the bundled PostgreSQL 18.6 (`pg_data` volume) |
+| `timescaledb` | `timescaledb` | the bundled TimescaleDB 2.31.0 on PostgreSQL 18.6 (`ts_data` volume) — never together with `postgres` |
+| `caddy` | `caddy` | the bundled Caddy reverse proxy with automatic HTTPS ([Reverse Proxy - Caddy](Reverse-Proxy-Caddy)) |
+
+| `COMPOSE_PROFILES=` | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `postgres` | bundled PostgreSQL, no proxy (the default) |
+| `postgres,caddy` | bundled PostgreSQL behind the bundled Caddy |
+| `timescaledb` / `timescaledb,caddy` | bundled TimescaleDB, without / with Caddy |
+| *(empty)* / `caddy` | your own PostgreSQL, without / with Caddy |
+
+Both bundled databases answer to the host name `db` inside the stack, so
+the app's settings don't change when you switch. Both are tuned for about
+100 machines out of the box; [Host Requirements](Host-Requirements#database-tuning)
+has the table of what to raise for 500, 1 000 or 5 000.
+
+**PostgreSQL or TimescaleDB?** PostgreSQL is the default and is fine for
+most fleets. TimescaleDB is PostgreSQL plus an extension for time-series
+data; pick it for a fleet of several hundred machines or more, or long
+monitoring retention. It is a drop-in: same version of PostgreSQL, same
+backups, and you can switch either way at any time. For now it stores the
+same tables as PostgreSQL; storing the monitoring history as compressed
+TimescaleDB hypertables is the next step.
+
+### Your own PostgreSQL
+
+Leave `postgres` and `timescaledb` out of `COMPOSE_PROFILES` and point the
+app at an existing PostgreSQL (16 or newer) in `.env`:
+
+```ini
+COMPOSE_PROFILES=            # or: caddy
+POSTGRES_HOST=pg.example.lan
+POSTGRES_PORT=5432
+POSTGRES_DB=debcontrol
+POSTGRES_USER=debcontrol
+POSTGRES_PASSWORD=…
+# or one URL instead of the five above:
+# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://debcontrol:…@pg.example.lan:5432/debcontrol
+```
+
+Create the user and an empty database owned by it first
+(`CREATE ROLE debcontrol LOGIN PASSWORD '…'; CREATE DATABASE debcontrol
+OWNER debcontrol;`); `migrate` creates the tables. The database must be
+reachable from the containers (not `localhost` — that is the container
+itself; use the host's LAN address, or `host.docker.internal` with an
+`extra_hosts` override). The `POSTGRES_*` tuning variables don't apply —
+tune your server yourself ([Host Requirements](Host-Requirements#database-tuning)
+has the numbers). `scripts/backup.sh` skips the database dump for an own
+database: back it up with your usual tools.
+
+### Switching between PostgreSQL and TimescaleDB
+
+The data moves by itself. Easiest:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/switch_db.sh timescaledb        # or: ./scripts/switch_db.sh postgres
+```
+
+It stops `web`/`worker`/`beat`, makes sure the old database is at the
+current migration, changes `COMPOSE_PROFILES` (keeping `caddy`), stops the
+old database and starts the stack. By hand works too: stop the stack,
+change `postgres` ↔ `timescaledb` in `COMPOSE_PROFILES`, `docker compose
+up -d` (and `docker compose stop db` / `stop timescaledb` if the old one is
+still running — Compose leaves a service of a switched-off profile
+running).
+
+How the automatic move works: when the stack starts, `migrate` creates the
+schema in the new database, then the one-off service `import-from-postgres`
+(or `import-from-timescaledb`) starts the old database from its volume
+inside its own container (local socket only, never on the network),
+copies every table and sequence over and marks the old volume as moved.
+`web`, `worker` and `beat` wait for it. It only ever copies into a new
+database nobody has used yet (no accounts) or one that was moved away from
+earlier; when both hold data of their own, it leaves both alone and says
+so. Follow it with `docker compose logs import-from-postgres`. It refuses,
+and the stack doesn't start, when
+
+- both databases are running (stop the one you are leaving),
+- the old database was not shut down cleanly (its `postmaster.pid` is
+  still there: stop it properly, or set `DEBCONTROL_DB_IMPORT_FORCE=1` in
+  `.env` for one start if you are sure it is not running),
+- the two are at different migrations (start the old profile once so it
+  is upgraded — `switch_db.sh` does this for you).
+
+The old volume (`debcontrol_pg_data` or `debcontrol_ts_data`) is kept,
+untouched, as a backup. Once you are happy, remove it:
+`docker volume rm debcontrol_pg_data`. Switching back later moves the
+newer data back the same way.
+
